  agriculture as in any industry hurt the  american people  producers consumers and workers alike  and they hurt the american economy  too many industries have become too  consolidated over time  too many companies have pursued  corporate conduct and more aggressive  mergers that have made all of us  vulnerable  against this background our antitrust  efforts cannot and will not slow down  but it is a fact that our anti-trust  division has been underfunded for too  long and has fewer staff today than it  had in the 1970s  that is why we are urging congress to  allocate the resources we need to  reinvigorate our enforcement efforts  and ensure a competitive economy for all  americans  i look forward to our work together in  the days ahead  thank you very much  thank you mr attorney general and uh mr  president uh if you may we're gonna uh  hear from a uh the uh group that we have  assembled here which represents the  enormous uh potential and uh diversity  uh geographic and otherwise in this uh  in this industry um and we're going to  start here with uh scott uh scott puba  who is the president of the oklahoma  farm farmers union uh and operates a uh  cattle rat ranch in oklahoma and i just  uh scott opened it to you  and would be curious to hear how you  approach the issue of  the options for processing  and what what it would mean to you to  have more options and how you think  about that in the context of the action  steps that we're trying to put forward  today  well thank you uh  thank you mr president for taking on  this  very critical issue for uh rural america  um  you know  this is on our minds of all of our  farmers and ranchers throughout the  country  and not not since teddy roosevelt had we  have a president that's willing to take  on  this big issue so thank you very much  for that  as uh to answer your question what will  it do for me as a farmer and rancher and  my membership  here in oklahoma and and the american  farmers and ranchers  is if we are able to uh be able to  process our animals  uh on a more local level  we can retain more of that retail food  dollar  in our at our farm gates and our farms  and ranches in our family operations  and in our rural communities uh for too  long we have seen multinational  meat packers suck out all of the wealth  of rural america and put it in their  corporate coffers and in some cases even  overseas  so we're excited about being able to  have  local processing done by local people  and then sell directly to the consumer  um  you know whether we can sell to the  grocery stores the restaurants  or the consumers directly  all of these will will enable our rural  communities to be lifted out of poverty  and we have a great  poverty problem in rural america  so we're excited about that in oklahoma  i will tell you uh 10 million dollars of  the first round  of of the cares covet money was actually  allocated through our oklahoma  department of agriculture  to fund some  projects just as you're speaking of  so we had 196 applicants apply for  that 10 million dollars of care's money  40 applicants were  chosen  uh and were able to be funded with that  money and out of that we had 19 new  mom and pr pop small processors either  open up for business here in our state  or they were able to upgrade an old  facility to meet the new safety and  health standards  and be able to market that across state  lines  we also  have had great success working with our  tribal neighbors  here in uh in oklahoma's many of our  tribal organizations their governments  have  uh put new processing online or are in  the process of it  processing both their cattle and their  buffalo  and their excess capacity that these new  clients has created  has also given our independent farmers  and ranchers in the community  an opportunity to get their cattle  processed and marketed so we're very  very pleased with that  and look forward to everything i heard  here today  what scott said at the beginning was  that this was a critical issue for rural  america  and i think it's critical uh from two  perspectives that he raised one  the notion of keeping the dollars in the  rural communities where the dollars are  being and the wealth is being generated  for far too long we've had an extraction  economy in rural america where these  guys work 24 7 365 days a year raising  these cattle and then they transport  them hundreds of miles away and the  profits basically go thousands of miles  away  what scott's talking about is retaining  those profits in the small towns that's  going to create opportunities for  farmers to be able to more better afford  that machinery that's going to keep the  implement dealer in business they're  going to be able to  support that main street business that's  going to keep those retail stores  in business  and it also going to create jobs and i  think it's really important to emphasize  it's not just farmers who are going to  benefit from this it's also rural folks  looking for a decent job  and if we do this right with workforce  training uh with assistance we can have  good paying jobs to complement what's  happening on the farm so  this is this is an incredible thing just  so you understand how how diff dire this  is mr president and i'll finish with  this  i was in council bluffs iowa talking to  a farmer uh this summer  and he came up to me and he said uh mr  secretary thank you so much for doing  this he said i just sold a load of  cattle  and he said this is what irritates me  about this whole system  i lost a hundred and fifty dollars ahead  150 ahead i lost on this on this cattle  but i know the processor that purchased  those cattle is going to make eighteen  hundred dollars ahead  and he looked at me and he said  mr secretary how is that fair  it's not  asking a question  scott  um when you talk about um  you said uh  um  be able to  go locally  how far  do you have to go before  these smaller operations opened up  to sell your cattle i me